    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$\\eta = \\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1), \\qquad [K_i,K_j] = -\\varepsilon_{ijk}L_k$$\n\n**One minus sign changes everything and nothing.** All of Stage 4's machinery — connection, geodesics, curvature, Bianchi — was derived without positive-definiteness, so it carries over untouched. What changes is the geometry: null vectors exist, and $(5,3,4,0)$ has zero length without being zero. In Euclidean signature that is impossible; here those vectors form a cone, and the cone *is* the causal structure.\n\n**The twin paradox is a sign.** From $(0,0)$ to $(2T,0)$, the straight worldline records $\\tau_A = 2T$ and the bent one $\\tau_B = 2T\\sqrt{1-v^2}$, less for every $v \\ne 0$ (at $v = 0.99$, 14% of the stay-at-home's). 4.03 proved geodesics locally *minimise* length; the same variational argument in Lorentzian signature makes them *maximise* proper time. Nothing is paradoxical — two curves, one metric, different lengths.\n\n**Rapidity adds; velocity does not.** $\\Lambda(\\varphi_1)\\Lambda(\\varphi_2) = \\Lambda(\\varphi_1+\\varphi_2)$ exactly, so boosts along an axis form a one-parameter subgroup. The velocity is $v = \\tanh\\varphi$, and\n\n$$\\tanh(\\varphi_1+\\varphi_2) = \\frac{v_1+v_2}{1+v_1v_2}$$\n\nwas verified identically. $0.99 \\oplus 0.99 = 0.99995$, and $1 \\oplus 0.5 = 1$: the speed of light is a **fixed point of the addition law**, not a limit imposed by hand. Time dilation is then just $\\cosh\\varphi = 1/\\sqrt{1-v^2}$.\n\n**Boosts do not close.** The three commutator blocks came out exactly:\n\n| bracket | result |\n|---|---|\n| $[L_i,L_j]$ | $+\\varepsilon_{ijk}L_k$ — rotations form a subalgebra |\n| $[L_i,K_j]$ | $+\\varepsilon_{ijk}K_k$ — boosts are a vector under rotation |\n| $[K_i,K_j]$ | $-\\varepsilon_{ijk}L_k$ — **boosts are not** |\n\nThat minus sign is the signature showing through: in $\\mathfrak{so}(4)$ it would be a plus. Its group-level consequence was checked by expanding $\\Lambda_x(as)\\Lambda_y(bs)\\Lambda_x(-as)\\Lambda_y(-bs)$ to $O(s^2)$ and matching $I - s^2ab\\,L_3$ exactly — a closed loop of pure boosts returns a **rotation**. That is Thomas precession, and it is holonomy (4.02) once more: go round a loop, come back turned, and the generator of the turn is a bracket.\n\n**$SL(2,\\mathbb C) \\to SO^+(1,3)$.** Writing $v$ as the Hermitian matrix $X = \\begin{pmatrix} t+z & x-iy\\\\ x+iy & t-z\\end{pmatrix}$ makes $\\det X = -\\langle v,v\\rangle$: **the Minkowski norm is a determinant**. Then $\\det(AXA^\\dagger) = |\\det A|^2\\det X$ identically (verified with explicit real and imaginary parts — declaring the entries real lets Maxima silently drop the conjugation and prove only the $SL(2,\\mathbb R)$ case), so $SL(2,\\mathbb C)$ acts by Lorentz transformations. $\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\varphi/2},e^{-\\varphi/2})$ reproduces $\\exp(\\varphi K_3)$ exactly, and $\\pm A$ give the same transformation: a **double cover**, exactly as $SU(2) \\to SO(3)$ in 1.03. Dimensions agree, $\\dim_{\\mathbb R}\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb C) = 6 = \\dim\\mathfrak{so}(1,3)$.\n\n**Where this connects.** 5.04 turned $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ into $\\mathfrak{so}(2,1)$ by $\\mathfrak m \\mapsto i\\mathfrak m$ and called the result a duality. This is the same complexification, taken seriously as the geometry of a space. The sphere, the hyperbolic plane and Minkowski space are three faces of one algebra.\n\n## Next\n\n**6.02 — Schwarzschild and the Tests of GR** puts curvature back in. 4.05 already computed $\\mathrm{Ric} = 0$ for the Schwarzschild metric with 24 non-zero Riemann components; this finishes the job — light bending, perihelion precession, and the event horizon as a coordinate artefact rather than a singularity.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Callahan, J.J. (2000) *The Geometry of Spacetime: An Introduction to Special and General Relativity*. New York: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0). Chapters 1–3 are special relativity done as geometry, at exactly this level, and the treatment of rapidity as the natural parameter is the one followed here.\n- ★ Landau, L.D. and Lifshitz, E.M. (1975) *The Classical Theory of Fields*. 4th revised English edn. Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2. Oxford: Pergamon. Chapters 1–2. Terse and unimprovable on the kinematics; §6 for proper time as the thing a clock reads.\n- O'Neill, B. (1983) *Semi-Riemannian Geometry with Applications to Relativity*. New York: Academic Press. The reference that develops Stages 3–4 in arbitrary signature from the start, so nothing has to be re-derived. Chapter 5 for causal character.\n- Naber, G.L. (2012) *The Geometry of Minkowski Spacetime*. 2nd edn. New York: Springer. Chapter 1 for the causal structure axiomatically, and Chapter 3 for the $SL(2,\\mathbb C)$ spinor picture in full.\n- Penrose, R. and Rindler, W. (1984) *Spinors and Space-Time*, vol. 1. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. Where the Hermitian-matrix construction of the last cell is the *starting point* rather than an aside.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed. Two Maxima notes: `trigsimp` does not know the hyperbolic addition formulae, so `exponentialize` then `ratsimp` is used throughout; and the coordinates are named `tv,xv,yv,zv` rather than `t,x,y,z` because assigning `x` or `y` breaks `plot2d`'s range specification — a trap this repo has hit twice before.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
